-- Both vendored packages are their Hackage releases with small patches, each marked
-- "Patched for Borrowed Light" in the source:
--   gloss-rendering 1.13.2.1: a minimised window (reported as 0x0) no longer aborts.
--     Faster drawing, the same pixels: OpenGL's colour and matrix mode are no
--     longer queried at every node; convex polygons are drawn in batches, one
--     glDrawArrays each, not one glBegin/glEnd per polygon; the convexity test
--     answers as before in a third of the time; GLU's triangles for non-convex
--     polygons are kept from one frame to the next.
--   gloss 1.13.2.2, GLFW backend only: key repeats are ignored, as in its GLUT
--     backend; letter keys go down and up by position, so they cannot stick on
--     non-QWERTY layouts; GLUT is initialised once on Linux, not twice.
--   gloss 1.13.2.2, play: a minor garbage collection after each frame, and a major
--     one at most once a second, not a major one that copied the whole heap every
--     frame.
-- Both directories must be committed with the project, or a clone cannot build.
packages: . vendor/gloss-rendering-1.13.2.1 vendor/gloss-1.13.2.2
with-compiler: ghc-9.6.7

-- Draw through GLFW, whose main loop is Haskell, instead of freeglut's C main loop:
-- on Windows the game crashed inside freeglut's callbacks into Haskell. GLFW is
-- compiled in from bindings-GLFW. Gloss only initialises GLUT on Linux, so Windows
-- and macOS no longer need freeglut at run time; Linux still needs it installed.
package gloss
  flags: -glut +glfw
